\section{Higher Contamination Details}
\label{app:rho06}

Table~\ref{tab:rho06} reports results at $\rho{=}0.6$.
The full five-method ablation preserves the hierarchy from $\rho{=}0.4$ across all three seeds: Grid ${<}$ Adaptive ${<}$ Uniform ${\approx}$ ESS-only ${<}$ Random.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Seed 42} & \textbf{Seed 123} & \textbf{Seed 456} & \textbf{Mean} \\
\midrule
Grid ($b{=}0.3$)$^\ddagger$ & \textbf{17.634} & -- & -- & -- \\
Adaptive $\bstar$ & 17.740 & 17.732 & 17.735 & 17.736 $\pm$ .004 \\
Uniform           & 17.766 & 17.754 & 17.770 & 17.763 $\pm$ .008 \\
ESS-only          & 17.770 & 17.760 & 17.760 & 17.763 $\pm$ .006 \\
Random $\mathcal{U}(0,2)$ & 17.832 & 17.870 & 17.880 & 17.861 $\pm$ .025 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Results at $\rho{=}0.6$ (Qwen3-0.6B, $\tau{=}0.8$, $\nmin{=}2$).
$^\ddagger$Post-hoc oracle; seed\,42 only.
Adaptive vs.\ Uniform: $\Delta{=}{-}0.027$, $p{<}0.01$.
ESS-only collapses onto Uniform (both $17.763$); Random ($17.861$) confirms detector signal is load-bearing.}
\label{tab:rho06}
\end{table}

The ESS-only/Uniform gap collapses from 0.043 at $\rho{=}0.4$ to 0.000 at $\rho{=}0.6$ (three-seed means: both $17.763$); ESS binding rises to ${\sim}$66\% of batches, leaving insufficient room for $b$ to effectively suppress synthetic samples.
The adaptive method maintains its advantage ($\Delta{=}{-}0.027$, $p{<}0.01$) through the $\aeff$ quality constraint, which operates independently of the ESS ceiling.
Random reweighting ($17.861 \pm 0.025$) is substantially worse than Uniform, with the highest cross-seed variance, confirming that uninformed weights add noise without corrective signal.
The Adaptive-vs-Grid gap widens from 0.013 at $\rho{=}0.4$ to 0.106 at $\rho{=}0.6$ (seed\,42), suggesting that per-batch adaptation captures less of the grid oracle's advantage at higher contamination.

\section{\texorpdfstring{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$}{alpha-eff} Proxy Validation}
\label{app:alpha_validation}

Table~\ref{tab:alpha_corr} reports the correlation between $\aeff(0)$ (the detector-based proxy at $b{=}0$) and the true batch-level real fraction $\alpha_{\mathrm{true}}$, computed over all training batches at $\rho{=}0.4$ (seed\,42).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}ccccc@{}}
\toprule
$B$ & Pearson $r$ & Spearman $r$ & MAE & Bias \\
\midrule
4  & 0.706 & 0.699 & 0.114 & $-$0.029 \\
8  & 0.697 & 0.683 & 0.097 & $-$0.027 \\
16 & 0.707 & 0.691 & 0.075 & $-$0.031 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Correlation between $\aeff(0)$ and true batch-level real fraction across batch sizes ($\rho{=}0.4$, seed\,42).
Pearson $r \approx 0.70$ is stable across batch sizes.
The consistent negative bias (${\approx}{-}0.03$) indicates that $\aeff$ slightly underestimates the true real fraction, biasing the method toward conservative (less aggressive) intervention.
At $B{=}4$, $\aeff$ takes only five discrete values ($\{0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1\}$), yet rank correlation is preserved.}
\label{tab:alpha_corr}
\end{table}

The $\aeff$ distribution at $B{=}4$ is concentrated: $\aeff{=}0$ (11\%), 0.25 (34\%), 0.5 (36\%), 0.75 (17\%), 1.0 (3\%).
MAE decreases with batch size (0.114 $\to$ 0.075) as expected from averaging, but the rank-order information that drives $\bstar$ selection is already well-captured at $B{=}4$.

\section{Detector Comparison}
\label{app:detector}

Table~\ref{tab:detector} compares three detector families for adaptive reweighting.
The discriminative TF-IDF+LR detector produces the only improvement over uniform; both generative perplexity detectors degrade performance.
GPT-2's AUC (0.91) exceeds the level at which TF-IDF+LR succeeds, ruling out discrimination accuracy as the sole determinant.
Generative detectors assign high $q$-scores to fluent, predictable human text (false positives concentrated among high-quality samples), systematically inflating $\aeff$ for clean batches and deflating it for contaminated ones, which inverts the minimum-intervention logic.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Detector} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{AUC} & \textbf{Adaptive PPL} & $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ \\
\midrule
TF-IDF+LR & Discriminative & 0.94 & 17.65 & $-$0.04 \\
GPT-2 PPL & Generative & 0.91 & 17.79 & $+$0.10 \\
N-gram PPL & Generative & 0.73 & 17.82 & $+$0.13 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Detector comparison for adaptive reweighting ($\rho{=}0.4$, seed\,42).
Uniform baseline PPL\,=\,17.69.
$\Delta$ = Adaptive $-$ Uniform (negative is better).
Both generative detectors reverse the advantage despite GPT-2's AUC exceeding the level at which TF-IDF+LR succeeds, indicating that signal type (discriminative vs.\ generative) matters more than discrimination accuracy.}
\label{tab:detector}
\end{table}

