\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

Let $\mathcal{D} = \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{real}} \cup \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{synth}}$ denote a pretraining corpus containing both human-authored and machine-generated text.
The contamination ratio $\rho = |\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{synth}}| / |\mathcal{D}|$ is unknown, a realistic assumption for web-scale corpora where synthetic content enters from diverse, uncoordinated sources~\citep{cheng2025contamination}.

A pre-trained detector assigns each sample $x$ a synthetic probability $q(x) \in [0,1]$.
Following \citet{drayson2025detection}, we weight each sample as
\begin{equation}
  w(x) = (1 - q(x))^{b},
  \label{eq:weight}
\end{equation}
where $b \geq 0$ controls downweighting intensity: $b = 0$ recovers uniform training, while larger $b$ progressively suppresses high-$q$ samples.
\citet{drayson2025detection} select $b$ via validation-set grid search, obtaining different optimal values for different model scales ($b{=}10$ for ${\leq}360$M parameters, $b{=}1$ for 1.7B).
We instead derive $b$ per-batch from the batch's own $q$-score distribution, requiring no held-out validation data.

\subsection{Dual-Constraint Framework}
\label{sec:dual}

Given a batch $\mathcal{B} = \{x_1, \ldots, x_B\}$ with detector scores $q_i = q(x_i)$ and weights $w_i(b) = (1 - q_i)^{b}$, we define two batch-level diagnostics that jointly determine $b$.

\paragraph{Effective real proportion.}
Let $r_i = \mathbf{1}[q_i \leq 0.5]$ estimate whether sample $x_i$ is human-authored.
The \emph{effective real proportion}
\begin{equation}
  \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{B} w_i(b) \cdot r_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{B} w_i(b)}
  \label{eq:alpha}
\end{equation}
measures the share of total weight allocated to likely-real samples.
The indicator $r_i = \mathbf{1}[q_i \leq 0.5]$ is a proxy for true provenance: $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ tracks the detector's estimate of the real fraction, not the ground truth, so all guarantees hold modulo detector quality.
Empirically, $\aeff$ correlates with the true batch-level real fraction at Pearson $r \approx 0.70$ with a conservative bias of $-0.03$ (Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_validation}), meaning the method tends toward less intervention rather than more.
Post-hoc calibration~\citep{guo2017calibration} reduces the gap between $q$-scores and true posteriors; in our experiments, Platt scaling yields ECE\,=\,0.048 (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).
At $b = 0$, $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ equals the raw (estimated) real fraction in the batch; increasing $b$ shifts weight toward low-$q$ samples, raising $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$.

\paragraph{Effective sample size.}
Kish's effective sample size~\citep{kish1965survey}
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{ESS}(b) = \frac{\bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{B} w_i(b)\bigr)^{2}}{\sum_{i=1}^{B} w_i(b)^{2}}
  \label{eq:ess}
\end{equation}
quantifies how many equally-weighted samples the weighted batch is equivalent to.
$\mathrm{ESS}(0) = B$; as $b$ grows, a few high-$(1{-}q)$ samples dominate and $\mathrm{ESS}$ drops toward~1.

\paragraph{Monotonicity.}
The two diagnostics exhibit opposing monotonicity in $b$:

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:monotone}
$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ is non-decreasing in $b$, and strictly increasing whenever the batch contains at least one sample with $q_i \in (0,1)$ and $r_i$ is not constant.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $\pi_i(b) = w_i / \sum_j w_j$ denote normalized weights.
Differentiating $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}} = \sum_i \pi_i r_i$ yields
$\frac{d\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}}{db} = \mathrm{Cov}_{\pi}\!\bigl(r_i,\, \ln(1{-}q_i)\bigr)$.
Real samples ($r_i = 1$) have $q_i \leq 0.5$ and thus higher $\ln(1{-}q_i)$, so this covariance is non-negative for any detector with better-than-random discrimination.
Full derivation in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
\end{proof}

\noindent $\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ is monotonically decreasing in $b$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:ess_mono}, Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}): larger exponents concentrate the weight distribution, a standard property of exponential tilting~\citep{kish1965survey,owen2013mc}.

\paragraph{Dual constraints.}
We impose two requirements on $b$:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Quality floor}: $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \geq \tau$.
  Convergence under contaminated training improves with the effective real fraction~\citep{wang2026survive}; $\tau$ sets the minimum acceptable quality level.
  By Proposition~\ref{prop:monotone}, this constraint defines a \emph{lower bound} $b_\tau = \inf\{b \geq 0 : \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \geq \tau\}$.
  \item \textbf{Stability ceiling}: $\mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}$.
  Overly concentrated weights yield high-variance gradients; $n_{\min}$ prevents the batch from degenerating into a single effective sample.
  Since $\mathrm{ESS}$ is decreasing, this defines an \emph{upper bound} $b_{\mathrm{ESS}} = \sup\{b \geq 0 : \mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}\}$.
\end{enumerate}
The optimal exponent is
\begin{equation}
  b^{*} = \inf\bigl\{b \geq 0 : \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \geq \tau\bigr\} \;\;\text{s.t.}\;\; \mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}.
  \label{eq:bstar}
\end{equation}
This is the \emph{minimum intervention principle}: apply the smallest $b$ that achieves the quality floor.
Borderline samples (those with moderate $q$-values) still carry useful training signal; over-downweighting discards this signal unnecessarily.
When both constraints are satisfiable ($b_\tau \leq b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$), $b^{*} = b_\tau$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is the binding constraint, and $b^{*}$ lies well below the ESS ceiling.
When they conflict ($b_\tau > b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$), the stability ceiling takes precedence: $b^{*} = b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$, accepting a lower $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ to preserve gradient stability.
The three regimes (clean, $\aeff$-binding, ESS-binding) partition the training batches; we quantify their relative frequencies in \S\ref{sec:regime}.

\subsection{Algorithm}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:bstar} computes $b^{*}$ for each training batch via bisection on the monotone functions $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\mathrm{ESS}$.
Each batch falls into one of three regimes:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Clean} ($\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(0) \geq \tau$): the quality floor holds at $b = 0$; no reweighting needed.
  \item \textbf{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$-binding} ($b_\tau \leq b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$): the quality constraint determines $b^{*} = b_\tau$; $\mathrm{ESS}$ remains above $n_{\min}$ with margin.
  \item \textbf{ESS-binding} ($b_\tau > b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$): the stability ceiling caps $b^{*} = b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$.
\end{enumerate}
Across 25{,}991 training micro-batches in our main experiment ($\rho{=}0.4$, $\tau{=}0.8$, $n_{\min}{=}2$), only ${\sim}2\%$ are clean; among reweighted batches, 38.2\% are $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$-binding and 61.8\% are ESS-binding.
In $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$-binding batches, the solver selects $b^{*} = 1.19$ on average (median 0.80), while an ESS-only strategy would choose $b = 5.88$ (median 3.59) on the same batches, 4.9$\times$ more aggressive (mean ratio).
The residual $\mathrm{ESS} = 2.60$ in these batches (vs.\ $n_{\min} = 2.0$) confirms that the dual constraint prevents unnecessary over-downweighting.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Per-batch adaptive $b^{*}$ selection}
\label{alg:bstar}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Batch scores $\{q_i\}_{i=1}^{B}$, indicators $\{r_i\}$, quality threshold~$\tau$, ESS floor~$n_{\min}$, search bound~$b_{\max}$, tolerance~$\varepsilon$
\ENSURE Optimal exponent $b^{*}$
\IF{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(0) \geq \tau$}
  \STATE \textbf{return} $0$ \COMMENT{clean batch}
\ENDIF
\STATE $b_\tau \gets \textsc{Bisect}(\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(\cdot){=}\tau,\; [0, b_{\max}],\; \varepsilon)$
\IF{$\mathrm{ESS}(b_\tau) \geq n_{\min}$}
  \STATE \textbf{return} $b_\tau$ \COMMENT{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$-binding}
\ELSE
  \STATE $b_{\mathrm{ESS}} \gets \textsc{Bisect}(\mathrm{ESS}(\cdot){=}n_{\min},\; [0, b_{\max}],\; \varepsilon)$
  \STATE \textbf{return} $b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$ \COMMENT{ESS-binding}
\ENDIF
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Implementation.}
Given $b^{*}$, we compute normalized weights $\pi_i = w_i(b^{*}) / \sum_j w_j(b^{*})$ and form the weighted loss $\mathcal{L} = \sum_i \pi_i \ell_i$, where $\ell_i$ is the per-sample cross-entropy.
This \emph{loss reweighting} maintains continuous gradients, unlike the SIR resampling of \citet{drayson2025detection} which introduces discrete sampling noise by drawing a subset proportional to $w_i$.
Each bisection requires $\mathcal{O}(B \log(b_{\max}/\varepsilon))$ operations; with $B{=}4$, $b_{\max}{=}20$, and $\varepsilon{=}0.01$, roughly 44 floating-point operations per batch, negligible beside the forward and backward passes.

\subsection{Hyperparameters}
\label{sec:hyperparameters}

The framework introduces two hyperparameters, both with principled defaults set once across all experiments.

\paragraph{Quality threshold $\tau$.}
Convergence theory for contaminated training shows that convergence rate improves with the effective real fraction~\citep{wang2026survive}, motivating a lower bound on $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$.
The specific value of $\tau$ is set empirically.
We find a wide safe range: $\tau \in [0.5, 0.8]$ consistently outperforms uniform training (Section~\ref{sec:results}), with $\tau = 0.8$ achieving the best perplexity.
Above 0.8, $b^{*}$ inflates rapidly (mean $b^{*} = 3.08$ at $\tau = 0.95$), causing over-downweighting.
We use $\tau = 0.8$ as the default throughout.

\paragraph{ESS floor $n_{\min}$.}
We recommend $n_{\min} = \max(2, \lfloor B/2 \rfloor)$ as a default that scales with batch size without manual tuning.
At this ratio, the ESS ceiling binds on a majority of batches (providing gradient stability) while leaving room for $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ to determine $b^{*}$ in the remainder (enabling quality-driven intervention).
Ratios below ${\sim}1/4$ effectively remove the stability constraint ($n_{\min}{=}1$, $B{=}4$: mean $b^{*}$ rises to 7.20, PPL degrades by $+$0.14), while $n_{\min}/B{=}1$ prevents intervention entirely ($b^{*} \equiv 0$, recovering uniform training).
This default is validated at $B \in \{4, 8, 16\}$ with PPL differences ${<}0.01$ (Section~\ref{sec:sensitivity}); we leave fine-grained ratio characterization (e.g., $0.4$ vs.\ $0.6$) to future work.
A practical caveat: ESS is computed per micro-batch (single forward pass), so $n_{\min}$ must satisfy $n_{\min} \leq B_{\mathrm{micro}}$; setting it relative to the effective batch (after gradient accumulation) silently forces $b^{*}{=}0$ on every step.

\paragraph{Why minimum intervention works.}
Restraint in downweighting outperforms aggressiveness.
In the 38.2\% of batches where $\aeff$ determines $\bstar$, the selected exponent is 4.9$\times$ lower than the ESS-only upper bound (\S\ref{sec:regime}), preventing unnecessary suppression of borderline samples ($q \approx 0.4$--$0.6$); this rate is robust across contamination levels (33.4\% at $\rho{=}0.6$) and seeds (CV ${<}2\%$).
$\aeff$ correlates with the true batch-level real fraction at Pearson $r \approx 0.70$ with a conservative bias of $-0.03$ (Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_validation}).

\paragraph{Where the advantage manifests.}
The advantage appears entirely at evaluation (PPL 17.65 vs.\ 17.69); training loss curves overlap (${\sim}1.4\%$ difference), consistent with reshaping the effective distribution.
Downstream benchmarks show 4 of 5 tasks with small negative $\Delta$ ($p \approx 0.19$, Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}); the aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL no better than uniform (17.693 vs.\ 17.694), confirming that per-batch adaptation is essential.

\paragraph{$\tau$ as a practical dial.}
$\tau \in [0.5, 0.8]$ all outperform uniform (Table~\ref{tab:tau}); sensitivity is asymmetric (over-cleaning degrades sharply, under-cleaning is mild), and $\tau \in [0.7, 0.8]$ is robust across $\rho \in \{0.4, 0.6\}$ (Appendix~\ref{app:tau_rho}).

\paragraph{Detector signal semantics.}
The method requires a discriminative detector: both generative alternatives (GPT-2 PPL at AUC\,=\,0.91, $\Delta{=}{+}0.10$; N-gram PPL at AUC\,=\,0.73, $\Delta{=}{+}0.13$) reverse the advantage despite GPT-2's high AUC, because they conflate predictability with synthetic origin and assign high $q$-scores to fluent human text.
TF-IDF+LR (AUC\,=\,0.94, ECE\,=\,0.048) succeeds because its features target provenance rather than fluency.

\paragraph{Filtering vs.\ reweighting.}
The 3-seed epoch-matched experiment (\S\ref{sec:main-results}) resolves the apparent D\&R advantage: at equal content epochs, D\&R ($17.657 \pm 0.006$) and adaptive $\bstar$ ($17.651 \pm 0.007$) are equivalent ($\Delta{=}0.006$).
D\&R's best full-length PPL (17.250 at $q{>}0.3$) requires ${\sim}$3.2$\times$ effective training compute and a calibrated threshold: the raw U-shaped curve is entirely a multi-epoch artifact, as epoch-matched comparisons show monotonically decreasing PPL that saturates near the adaptive level (Figure~\ref{fig:dr_dual_curve}).
Reweighting sidesteps this tradeoff: no threshold to calibrate, no data discarded, and the parity at equal epochs ($\Delta{=}0.006$) suggests soft provenance-based weighting substitutes for hard filtration without information loss.

